      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
        <div class="blog-prose">
          <h2 id="how-pf-is-calculated">How PF is calculated</h2>

          <p>Provident Fund is calculated on <strong>Basic salary plus Dearness Allowance</strong>, not on gross salary. This is the single most common source of confusion — an employee with ₹50,000 gross and ₹20,000 Basic has PF calculated on ₹20,000, not ₹50,000.</p>

          <p>The standard rates are <strong>12% from the employee</strong> and <strong>12% from the employer</strong>. A statutory wage ceiling of <strong>₹15,000 per month</strong> applies, and most employers cap the contribution base there. At the capped base, the employee deduction works out to ₹1,800 per month.</p>

          <h3>The employer's 12% is split in two</h3>

          <p>The employee's contribution goes entirely into the Provident Fund. The employer's does not. It divides between the Employees' Pension Scheme and the Provident Fund:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>EPS</strong> — 8.33% of the pensionable wage, which is always capped at ₹15,000 even if you contribute PF on full Basic. That puts the maximum EPS allocation at ₹1,250 per month.</li>
            <li><strong>EPF</strong> — the balance of the employer's 12% after the EPS allocation.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>This is why the calculator shows the employer total split into two lines. On a capped base of ₹15,000: employer total ₹1,800, EPS ₹1,250, EPF balance ₹550.</p>

          <h3>Contributing on full Basic</h3>

          <p>Employers may contribute on Basic above ₹15,000 rather than capping at the ceiling. Tick the option in the calculator to model it. Note what changes and what does not: the PF portion rises with the higher base, but the EPS allocation stays pinned to the ₹15,000 ceiling. The extra contribution flows entirely into the Provident Fund, not the pension.</p>

          <h3>What this calculator does not include</h3>

          <p>Employers also pay administrative charges and EDLI insurance on top of the 12%, both calculated on the capped wage. Those are employer costs rather than employee deductions, so they are excluded here. Voluntary Provident Fund, establishment-specific exclusions, international workers and employees who were already above the ceiling before joining EPF all follow different treatment.</p>

          <h2 id="pf-on-the-payslip">Where PF appears on the payslip</h2>

          <p>Only the employee's 12% is a payslip deduction. The employer's 12% is a cost to the company that never reduces take-home pay — though it usually appears in the CTC breakup, which is why CTC is always higher than what lands in the bank account. If you are working backwards from a CTC figure, the <a href="<%= ctc_calculator_path %>">CTC to in-hand calculator</a> models both sides.</p>

          <p>For where PF sits in the wider month-end sequence — attendance lock, gross pay, the other three statutory deductions and the deposit deadlines — see the <a href="<%= blog_post_path("payroll-processing-india") %>">complete guide to payroll processing in India</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%= render "resources/calculator_footer",
